{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.TraceRealSpectrumComplex","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.TraceRealSpectrumComplex","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Real","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.isElliptic_det_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.isElliptic_det_pos","kind":"theorem","line":95,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TraceRealSpectrumComplex.lean#L95-L101","doc":"An elliptic pair has strictly positive determinant, so its eigenvalues have\na well-defined common modulus `√det` and the eigenphase `θ` satisfies\n`cos θ = tr / (2√det)`. Recorded so the reported `θ = 2.020445` has its\ndefinition next to it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.short_model_not_elliptic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.short_model_not_elliptic","kind":"theorem","line":87,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TraceRealSpectrumComplex.lean#L87-L93","doc":"And the short model is **not** elliptic, on the same measured data\n(`tr ≈ 0`, `det ≈ −0.0109`) — so the two models genuinely differ in kind, not\njust in value. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.real_trace_real_det_but_complex_spectrum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.real_trace_real_det_but_complex_spectrum","kind":"theorem","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TraceRealSpectrumComplex.lean#L75-L85","doc":"**The correction, formally.** There is a real trace and a real determinant\nwhose matrix has a genuinely complex spectrum. So \"every trace in the tower is\nreal\" cannot decide whether a tower operator is elliptic — which is precisely\nwhere Step 21 over-reached.\n\nThe witness is `Δ_L`'s measured `(2,1)` block, rounded: `tr = −0.0914`,\n`det = 0.0110`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.isElliptic_iff_traceless_det_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.isElliptic_iff_traceless_det_pos","kind":"theorem","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TraceRealSpectrumComplex.lean#L68-L73","doc":"Equivalently, the traceless part has positive determinant — the `det A > 0`\nthat `HolonomySpectrumSignObstruction` requires. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.IsElliptic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.IsElliptic","kind":"def","line":62,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TraceRealSpectrumComplex.lean#L62-L66","doc":"A real `2 × 2` matrix is **elliptic** — complex-conjugate spectrum — exactly\nwhen its characteristic discriminant `tr² − 4·det` is negative. Stated on the\nscalars so it needs no matrix machinery: the eigenvalues solve\n`x² − (tr)x + det = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}